```java
// Old way — error-prone
BufferedReader br = null;
try {
    br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("file.txt"));
    return br.readLine();
} finally {
    if (br != null) br.close(); // can forget or throw
}

// Try-with-resources — clean
try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("file.txt"))) {
    return br.readLine();
} // br.close() called automatically
```

The `AutoCloseable` interface has exactly one method:
```java
public interface AutoCloseable {
    void close() throws Exception;
}
```

`Closeable` (java.io) is a subinterface:
```java
public interface Closeable extends AutoCloseable {
    void close() throws IOException;
}
```

All Java I/O classes (`InputStream`, `OutputStream`, `Reader`, `Writer`, `Connection`, etc.) implement `Closeable`, which means they work with try-with-resources automatically. You can also make your own classes work by implementing `AutoCloseable`.

```java
class DatabaseConnection implements AutoCloseable {
    public DatabaseConnection() { System.out.println("opened"); }

    @Override
    public void close() { System.out.println("closed"); }
}

try (DatabaseConnection conn = new DatabaseConnection()) {
    // use conn
} // close() called automatically
```

```java
try (
    Connection conn = getConnection();      // opened 1st
    PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT 1") // opened 2nd
) {
    ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery();
    // use rs
} 
// Closed in REVERSE order:
// ps.close() first, then conn.close()
```

```java
try (
    MyResource r = new MyResource() // close() throws
) {
    r.doWork(); // also throws
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Primary: " + e.getMessage());
    for (Throwable s : e.getSuppressed()) {
        System.out.println("Suppressed: " + s.getMessage());
    }
}
```

```java
// Valid — no catch block
// close() still called; IOException propagates to caller
public void process() throws IOException {
    try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("f.txt"))) {
        System.out.println(br.readLine());
    }
    // br.close() called here automatically
}
```

